 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** Developed by Kim et al. for surgical simulators [S1, S2].
-*   **History:** Presented at the SPIE International Symposium on Medical Imaging in San Diego, CA, February 2000. Published online in Wiley Interscience in 2002/2003 [S1].
-*   **Usage:** Applied to a spine biopsy simulator and image-guided therapy surgical simulators [S1, S2].
+The method was developed for surgical simulators, specifically a spine biopsy simulator and image-guided therapy simulators [S1, S2]. It addresses the need for real-time visualization of 3D medical images (organs) and medical instrument motion [S1, S2]. Volume rendering is used for organs because segmentation is difficult and organs have fuzzy/complex structures, while surface rendering is used for instruments because they have simple structures and require fast motion updates [S1, S2]. The method builds on the shear-warp factorization technique, which transforms 3D data to an intermediate sheared space for efficient resampling and compositing [S2].
 
